    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that the ends of a beam are insulated and that there is a constant source term:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{t} \amp = \pdtwo{u}{x} + 1 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{x}(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{x}(L,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Thinking about the physical situation, explain why there should be no stead-state solution.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens mathematically when we try to find a steady-state solution?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We saw in a video that the total heat energy in a three-dimensional region <m>E</m> is
                    <md>
                        \iiint_E c\rho u (x,y,z,t)~dV
                    </md>
                    where <m>c</m> is the specific heat and <m>\rho</m> is the mass density.  If we 
                    apply this to a one-dimensional beam whose cross sectional area is <m>A</m>, we find the 
                    total heat energy
                    <md>
                        \int_0^L A c \rho u(x,t)~dx.
                    </md>
                    For convenience, we choose units so that <m>A = 1</m>, <m>c = 1</m>, and <m>\rho = 1</m> so
the total heat energy is just 
                    <md>
                        \int_0^L u(x,t)~dx.
                    </md>
                    By differentiating the expression above (for the total heat energy) with respect to time,
and applying the heat equation given in this problem, describe how the total heat energy is changing over time.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>u(x,t)</m> is a solution to the equation and boundary conditions, explain why
                    <m>{v(x,t) = u(x,t)-t}</m> solves a homogeneous equation with homogeneous boundary conditions.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The following Sturm-Liouville problem arises when we consider the homoogeneous one-
dimensional heat equation with the temperature fixed on one end and the other end insulated.
                <md>
                    <mrow>X'' \amp = -kX </mrow>
                    <mrow>X(0) \amp =0</mrow>
                    <mrow>X'(L) \amp =0 </mrow>
                </md>
                Note this is not something we've seen exactly before.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the Sturm-Liouville problem by finding the eigenvalues <m>k</m> and 
                    eigenfunctions <m>X(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw graphs of the first three eigenfunctions and verify that they satisfy the boundary
conditions.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the heat equation with boundary and initial conditions in a beam of length <m>L=1</m>.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{t} \amp = \pdtwo{u}{x} + 6 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(1,t) \amp = 8 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(x,0) \amp = 10 </mrow>
                </md>
                Recall, we first need to make it homogeneous by performing the trick <m>{v=u-u_{ss}}</m>. Be careful
- the initial condition for <m>v</m> is different then the initial condition for <m>u</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the heat equation with boundary and initial conditions in a beam of length <m>L=1</m>.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{t} \amp = \pdtwo{u}{x} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{x}(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\pd{u}{x}(1,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(x,0) \amp = x^2 </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    
   
</worksheet>